autolisp-spec-load-open-encoding-docs — document load/open file-encoding behavior (BOM, UTF-8/16, encoding arg)
==============================================================================================================

Type: documentation
Status: DONE
Reported from: SCHMS+ integration (alfe --bricscad on macOS), 2026-07-30

Summary
-------

The autolisp-spec entries for `load` and `open`, and the other
clautolisp encoding documentation, do not yet record the concrete
file-encoding rules the vendors document. These rules explain the
whole macOS cp1252 problem (see cad-chr-vs-loaded-encoding.issue and
cad-load-encoding-macos.issue) and must be specified so clautolisp/alfe
behavior can be defined against them.

Primary source to cite
-----------------------

BricsCAD Developer Reference (V25), `open`:
  https://developer.bricsys.com/bricscad/help/en_US/V25/DevRef/index.html?page=source%2Fopen.htm
  (content page: .../V25/DevRef/source/open.htm)
BricsCAD Developer Reference (V25), `load`:
  https://developer.bricsys.com/bricscad/help/en_US/V25/DevRef/index.html?page=source%2Fload.htm
  (content page: .../V25/DevRef/source/load.htm)

Facts to document
-----------------

1. `load` — Unicode source files (BricsCAD V20+)

   Signature: (load lspfile [failure-expression])
   Extension search when none given: name.des then name.lsp.
   Verbatim (BricsCAD DevRef):
     "Since BricsCAD V20, the (load) function also supports Lisp files
      which are stored as 'Unicode':
        - using UTF-8  format in 'Little-Endian' byte order
        - using UTF-16 format in 'Little-Endian' byte order
      For both UTF-8/16 format, the Byte-Order-Mark (BOM) is absolutely
      required; UTF-8 without BOM might fail."
   Note: `load` itself has NO encoding parameter; the encoding is
   detected from the BOM, otherwise the file is decoded with the host
   system codepage (SYSCODEPAGE). => A non-BOM cp1252 source cannot be
   portably native-loaded (on macOS SYSCODEPAGE=MAC_ROMAN, so it is
   mis-decoded).

2. `open` — explicit encoding argument (AutoCAD 2021+ / BricsCAD V21+)

   Signature: (open fileName openMode [encoding])
   Modes: "r" "w" "a" and binary "rb" "wb" "ab".
   Optional encoding argument, verbatim (BricsCAD DevRef):
     allowed values "utf8", "utf8-bom", "utf16-bom".
     "UTF-8 without BOM can not be distinguished from normal ANSII."
   Version: "introduced by AutoCAD 2021", "supported in BricsCAD V21
   and higher." Pre-2021 `open` had no encoding argument.

3. AutoCAD parity note

   AutoCAD 2021 introduced the same Unicode changes under `LISPSYS`;
   the encoding argument to `open` is the AutoCAD 2021 feature BricsCAD
   mirrors from V21. The pre-Unicode vs post-Unicode distinction is
   already sketched in the spec draft (see cross-references).

What to update
--------------

- autolisp-spec/documentation/autolisp-visual-lisp-specification-draft.org
  * Add proper function entries for `load` and `open` capturing the
    signatures above and the encoding/BOM rules, version-qualified.
  * The existing "Pre-Unicode and Post-Unicode Semantics" section
    (~line 4929) already notes "explicit file-opening encoding support"
    and "extended `open` modes" — expand it with the concrete argument
    values ("utf8"/"utf8-bom"/"utf16-bom") and the load BOM requirement,
    with the version qualifiers and the source URLs.

- Other clautolisp encoding docs to align with the same facts:
  * clautolisp/autolisp-file-compat/documentation/usage.org
    (open modes + encoding arg; how clautolisp maps them)
  * clautolisp/autolisp-runtime/documentation/autolisp-runtime-specifications.org
    (ascii/chr/read-char pre/post-Unicode behavior; codepage vs Unicode)
  * clautolisp/documentation/clautolisp-user-manual.org
    (any user-facing note on source encoding / SYSCODEPAGE)

Consistency to assert in the spec
---------------------------------

Whatever the file encoding, a decoded non-ASCII character must satisfy
`(= (chr N) <same char decoded from a file>)` — i.e. one canonical
internal representation per character (this is where BricsCAD's native
`chr` currently diverges from a file-decoded char; clautolisp already
does this right). Documenting the rule fixes the contract alfe must
honor when it hands sources to a CAD host (transcode to UTF-8+BOM).

