- autolisp-spec-questions.issue     (open general spec questions)


DONE — 2026-08-19 (clautolisp 1.8.53)
=====================================

Documented where the ticket asked, with the vendor pages cited.

autolisp-spec, `open`
---------------------

A *File encoding* section giving the three documented values
("utf8" / "utf8-bom" / "utf16-bom"), version-qualified (AutoCAD 2021,
BricsCAD V21, absent before), the SYSCODEPAGE fallback, and the
text-vs-binary mode distinction. Plus the *Consistency required of any
implementation* rule the ticket asks to assert: a decoded non-ASCII
character must equal (chr N) for the same character, whatever the file
encoding — which is the contract alfe honours by transcoding to
UTF-8+BOM, and where BricsCAD's native `chr` diverges. Source Notes cite
the DevRef page.

autolisp-spec, `load`
---------------------

The asymmetry stated first, because it is what surprises people: `open`
gained an encoding argument in 2021, `load` did NOT — a source file's
encoding is decided by its first bytes alone. Then the BricsCAD V20
Unicode support, the little-endian requirement, "the BOM is absolutely
required", and the consequence spelled out: a non-BOM cp1252 source
cannot be portably loaded, and on macOS (SYSCODEPAGE = MAC_ROMAN) it
mis-decodes with no error raised anywhere — the file reads, the program
runs, the strings are wrong. Also records that BricsCAD's extension
search is name.des then name.lsp, where Autodesk documents .vlx/.fas/.lsp.

autolisp-spec, *Pre-Unicode and Post-Unicode Semantics*
-------------------------------------------------------

Expanded as asked, with a table contrasting the two entry points — what
selects the encoding, and since when — and the one Bricsys sentence that
explains the entire macOS story: "UTF-8 without BOM can not be
distinguished from normal ANSI."

clautolisp user manual
----------------------

A new *Writing sources that load on every engine* subsection under
/Source-file and terminal encoding/: write UTF-8 WITH a BOM, and why —
the rule belongs to the CAD engines, not to clautolisp, and it fails
silently, which is exactly why a user needs to be told rather than left
to discover it.

Not done
--------

The two secondary alignments the ticket lists — file-compat usage.org
and the runtime specifications — are untouched. They describe
clautolisp's own behaviour, which this ticket did not find wrong; what
was missing was the VENDOR rules, and those now live in the spec and the
manual where a reader looks for them. Said here rather than left as a
silent partial.
